<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gedik Ahmed Pasha was an Ottoman grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and admiral under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. He conquered the Karamanid lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> in the early 1470s, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> and the Genoese colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1475, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1480. He was executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> in 1482.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little about Gedik Ahmed Pasha appears in primary sources, and it was late in historiography that more was learned of him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> were both proposed as his birthplace, and Mükrimin Halil Yinanç even claimed, from unnamed Western sources, that he descended from the Byzantine Palaiologos dynasty. Research in the Ottoman archives of Vranje, in south-eastern Serbia, by Aleksandar Stojanovski established that he belonged to the local Serbian feudal families and was born in the village of Punoševce. He served as grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and as kapudan pasha, Grand Admiral of the Ottoman Navy, under the sultans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Leading the Ottoman army, he defeated the Karamanids, the last Anatolian principality to resist Ottoman expansion in the region. They had been the strongest principality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> for nearly 200 years and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, the former Selçuk capital. He also fought the Venetians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. In 1475 the sultan sent him to help the Crimean Khanate against the Genoese. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Soldaia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaklava">Cembalo</a> and other Genoese castles, the Principality of Theodoro with its capital Mangup, and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and he rescued the khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray">Meñli I Giray</a>, from the Genoese. Crimea and Circassia thereby entered the Ottoman sphere of influence.</p><p>In 1479, as sanjakbey of Avlona, he was ordered to lead between 10,000 and 40,000 troops to the siege of Shkodra. Later that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> gave him command of the fleet in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and he conquered the islands of Santa Maura (Lefkada), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Kefalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zante</a> (Zakynthos).</p><p>Mehmed saw himself as the heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and seriously considered conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Gedik Ahmed was sent with a naval force to the heel of the peninsula. After a failed attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, held by the Knights of St. John, he took the harbour city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> in 1480. For lack of food and supplies he had to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> with most of his troops that year, planning to continue in 1481.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>'s death prevented the continuation of the campaign. In the struggle over the succession, Gedik Ahmed sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a>, who nevertheless did not fully trust him. Bayezid had him imprisoned and later killed on 18 November 1482.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gedik Ahmed Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1475 an Ottoman fleet under <b>Gedik Ahmed Pasha</b> conquered Caffa and Theodoro, and Mengli I Giray, restored to the throne with Ottoman support in 1478, accepted the suzerainty of the sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A word from the sultan could end a career: Bayezid II had the grand vizier <b>Gedik Ahmed Pasha</b> executed in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman commander <b>Gedik Ahmed Pasha</b> took Alanya in 1471 from its last ruler, Kilij Arslan, who received a domain elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaiye-beylik">Alaiye Beylik</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1475 an Ottoman fleet under <b>Gedik Ahmed Pasha</b> took Caffa and the other colonies, ending Italian rule in the Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Genoese Gazaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Serbian Despotate. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1472" aria-label="Edirne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gedik Ahmed Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1472">What was here in 1472? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
